[Student "S"]
{You would really like to have a transfer bid in this situation, to make East bid \Hs.

But you don't have one, so you just bid 4\H yourself.

West plays 4\H. North leads the \ST. You play low in dummy and North holds the trick. He continues the suit and South takes the \SQ and then plays the \SA.

Loser List: \S = 2 : \H = 1 : \D = 0 : \C = 1 :: Total = 4

You've already lost the 2 \Ss. If North has the \HK you might avoid a loser there. You don't have a winner on which to discard the \C loser. 

If South has the \HK it will have to be a singleton to help you, so it is more logical to play North for the \HK.  But if North does have the \HK it will do you no good to ruff South's \SA, North will over-ruff and you would still have that \C loser. 

So do it this way instead. Discard the \C loser on South's \SA! Another \S from South won't hurt you since dummy would be able to over-ruff North. Whatever South plays you can win, then finesse North out of his \HK.

It was said earlier that you didn't have a winner on which you could discard the \C loser.

That was true, so instead you were able to discard it on a loser! }
[Play "N"]
ST

